Description: BROWSE encoder becomes the waveform zoom on the Overview tab
 The BROWSE rotary is bound to [Library],MoveVertical, so it only ever
 scrolled the list -- there is no hardware way to zoom the waveforms at all,
 despite the stock mapping's own header advertising "Waveform zoom (shift)"
 for a binding that does not exist anywhere in its XML.
 .
 The encoder now follows the screen instead of picking one job for good: with
 the Overview tab up it zooms, and in Browse/Search it scrolls exactly as
 before. That keeps the only hardware route through a track list -- losing it
 on an appliance whose alternative is a touchscreen would have been a plain
 regression -- while putting zoom on the knob at the moment the waveforms are
 the only thing on screen.
 .
 The decision is made in LibraryControl::slotMoveVertical, i.e. at the
 *destination* of the stock binding, and deliberately NOT in res/controllers.
 The r31 version of this patch rebound the encoder to a <Script-Binding/> and
 did the work in Pioneer-DDJ-400-script.js; that never fired on hardware.
 Mixxx prefers a mapping of the same name from the user mapping directory over
 the one in /usr/share/mixxx/controllers (DlgPrefController consults the user
 enumerator last and lets its match win), and it writes a copy there whenever
 a mapping is dirty; <scriptfiles> are then resolved next to that XML. So a
 single stale ~/.mixxx/controllers/Pioneer-DDJ-400.midi.xml silently shadows
 both the patched mapping and the patched script, and no deb can fix that
 because no deb owns that directory. Doing it in C++ removes the dependency
 completely: the shipped binary decides, the mapping stays stock, and any
 input that reaches [Library],MoveVertical -- any controller, any mapping, and
 the Up/Down keys through slotMoveUp()/slotMoveDown() -- zooms on the Overview
 tab.
 .
 One detent is one zoom step, applied to every deck from a single target
 rather than stepping each from its own value: the skin stacks both waveforms
 permanently and they are only readable against each other at a common scale,
 so this also pulls them back together if they ever diverge. Clockwise zooms
 in, which means *subtracting*, because waveform_zoom counts how much track is
 on screen. The current level is rounded before stepping because the mouse
 wheel (WWaveformViewer::wheelEvent) leaves fractional zoom levels behind. The
 target is clamped to WaveformWidgetRenderer's s_waveformMinZoom..MaxZoom here
 because BaseTrackPlayerImpl::slotWaveformZoomValueChangeRequest ignores a
 request outside that range rather than clamping into it, so an unclamped
 target at either end would silently do nothing.
 .
 LibraryControl now creates [Tab],overview. The control is normally created by
 LegacySkinParser when it parses <SingletonContainer trigger="[Tab],overview">,
 and the parser reuses one that already exists, so this changes nothing about
 how the skin drives it -- but slotMoveVertical reads the key on every detent,
 and ControlObject::get() on a control that does not exist asserts in a debug
 build and warns otherwise. The key therefore has to exist even under a stock
 skin that never defines it; there it simply stays 0 and the encoder keeps its
 stock scrolling behaviour. Creating it in LibraryControl also gets the
 ordering right (CoreServices, and with it Library/LibraryControl, is
 constructed before the skin is parsed) and keeps it alive across a skin
 reload, which destroys every skin-created control.
 .
 Not changed: the encoder still zooms while the settings overlay is open,
 because that overlay deliberately blocks touch input only and leaves the
 hardware controls live, exactly like the jog and transport buttons.
